


If TICKS_PER_SECOND is 256, then one tick is 1/256 seconds.
Let's just use Hz: 256 Hz.
Our baud is 38,400 Hz.
Our cycles are 16,000,000 Hz.
So okay, we *definitely* need more ticks per second than our baud. O_o
In fact, let's say we want to guarantee a time slice (i.e. tick) for every task
in between UART operations, i.e. baud.
So we need TICKS_PER_SECOND to be at least BAUD * MAX_TASKS.
Currently, MAX_TASKS is 5.
Let's aim for 8 (if we get the stack size down, e.g. by giving interrupts their
own stack).
Then BAUD * MAX_TASKS would be 307,200.
So, how many cycles is that?..

    1/307,200 s = X cycles
    1 cycle = 1/16,000,000 s
    1/307,200 s = X * 1/16,000,000 s
    X = 1/307,200 * 16,000,000 = 52.083333333

Wow, that's it?.. 52 cycles per time slice...
And at one point, I estimated 16 cycles per tasklang instruction.
So 3.25 tasklang instructions per time slice.
That's... underwhelming. O_o
And that's not even taking into consideration the overhead from context
switching!.. which we will need to calculate at some point!..

Now, what might we do to improve the situation?..
For one thing, what if we loosen this requirement:

    say we want to guarantee a time slice (i.e. tick) for every task
    in between UART operations

The reason we wanted to do that was so that, if only one task was willing
to handle a given UART operation, that task would have a chance to "see"
the operation before another UART operation occurred, blowing away the
value from the previous operation.

But instead, what if we bring back the UART Rx interrupt?..
Ehhh what, and have it run the task?! No!.. actually we can bring back the
UART Rx interrupt, and have it choose a random "waiting for UART" task to
"wake up" by *changing the task's status*, but then we should still allow
the timer interrupt to be the thing which actually runs a time slice for each
task.

Okay, first of all, let's bring back read/write queues.

    #define DEFINE_QUEUE(QUEUE) \
    task_id_t QUEUE[MAX_TASKS]; \
    uint8_t QUEUE##_head = 0; \
    uint8_t QUEUE##_tail = 0;

    #define QUEUE_PUSH(QUEUE, VALUE) { \
        QUEUE[QUEUE##_tail++] = VALUE; \
        if (QUEUE##_tail >= MAX_TASKS) QUEUE##_tail = 0; \
    }

    #define QUEUE_POP(QUEUE, VALUE) { \
        VALUE = QUEUE[QUEUE##_head++]; \
        if (QUEUE##_head >= MAX_TASKS) QUEUE##_head = 0; \
    }

    DEFINE_QUEUE(read_queue)
    DEFINE_QUEUE(write_queue)

